1
00:00:07,799 --> 00:00:09,999
The magazine said yes in July.

2
00:00:10,569 --> 00:00:16,224
It was one of the three or four national magazines
left in America that still paid for

3
00:00:16,244 --> 00:00:21,833
long stories — twelve thousand words, six months of
reporting, a cover if it was good

4
00:00:21,853 --> 00:00:27,706
enough. They had read her pitch, and her Verdant Axis
series, and her notes on the eleven

5
00:00:27,726 --> 00:00:31,137
seconds, and they had called her
the same afternoon.

6
00:00:31,707 --> 00:00:37,667
Hannah Price quit the Wilmington Ledger the following
Monday. The newsroom, which by then

7
00:00:37,687 --> 00:00:43,378
had nine people in it, gave her a sheet cake with a
pair of black sunglasses drawn on

8
00:00:43,398 --> 00:00:49,090
it in icing. She ate one piece, and threw the
sunglasses in the bin, and did not know

9
00:00:49,110 --> 00:00:50,971
why that made her so uneasy.

10
00:00:53,042 --> 00:00:58,493
The first call came two weeks later, at twenty to
midnight, from a number she did not

11
00:00:58,513 --> 00:00:59,136
recognise.

12
00:00:59,826 --> 00:01:01,377
Is this Hannah Price?

13
00:01:01,898 --> 00:01:03,146
Who's asking?

14
00:01:03,665 --> 00:01:09,165
Someone who reads your work. I'm calling from a phone
I bought with cash at a gas station

15
00:01:09,185 --> 00:01:14,560
in Newark. I'm going to throw it in the river when
I'm done. Don't try to call it back.

16
00:01:15,879 --> 00:01:21,276
She knew his voice. She did not know how she knew it
— she had never spoken to him — but

17
00:01:21,296 --> 00:01:26,692
it was the voice of an educated man in his late
fifties, precise, frightened, and trying

18
00:01:26,712 --> 00:01:31,923
very hard to sound as though he was not. She had
heard a great many voices like it on

19
00:01:31,943 --> 00:01:37,216
a great many late-night calls. They were the voices
of people who had worked somewhere

20
00:01:37,236 --> 00:01:42,079
for a long time, and seen something, and been
unable to stop thinking about it.

21
00:01:42,769 --> 00:01:43,937
I'm listening.

22
00:01:44,456 --> 00:01:48,681
You're writing about him. The profile.
Everybody knows. He knows.

23
00:01:49,201 --> 00:01:50,832
That isn't a secret.

24
00:01:51,352 --> 00:01:56,467
No. Nothing is, with him. That's what I want to tell
you. Whatever you think you're seeing,

25
00:01:56,487 --> 00:01:58,274
he's already seen you seeing it.

26
00:01:59,795 --> 00:02:01,203
What do you want to tell me?

27
00:02:03,221 --> 00:02:05,195
Ask him about Control Seven.

28
00:02:05,716 --> 00:02:07,353
What's Control Seven?

29
00:02:07,873 --> 00:02:13,345
Ask him. Watch his face when you ask. And then —
then ask yourself why a man who wants

30
00:02:13,365 --> 00:02:17,560
the whole world to watch him never, ever
lets anyone see his eyes.

31
00:02:18,079 --> 00:02:19,421
The line went dead.

32
00:02:19,991 --> 00:02:25,511
In the parking lot of St. Anthony's church, Howard
Lessing sat for a long time with the

33
00:02:25,531 --> 00:02:31,304
phone in his lap, shaking. Then he drove to the river,
and threw it in, as he had promised.

34
00:02:31,324 --> 00:02:37,034
He drove home the long way, under the speed limit,
checking his mirrors every few seconds,

35
00:02:37,054 --> 00:02:42,573
and when he got home he sat in his car in the garage
for twenty minutes before he could

36
00:02:42,593 --> 00:02:44,292
bring himself to go inside.

37
00:02:45,864 --> 00:02:51,954
It had been nearly two years since the night in lab
two. The fear had not gone away. It

38
00:02:51,974 --> 00:02:58,275
had simply, very slowly, begun to decay — the way the
Adolf half of the mind had predicted

39
00:02:58,295 --> 00:03:04,385
it would — and in the space it left behind,
something older had begun to grow back. Not

40
00:03:04,405 --> 00:03:07,475
courage, exactly. Something
closer to spite.

41
00:03:08,045 --> 00:03:13,862
He had been careful. He had used cash. He had driven
to a church in a parish he did not

42
00:03:13,882 --> 00:03:19,229
belong to, in another county, and he had left his
own phone at home in a drawer.

43
00:03:19,800 --> 00:03:25,289
For the first time since Albert Wesker had arrived in
the world, somebody had done something

44
00:03:25,309 --> 00:03:26,606
that Hale did not see.

45
00:03:28,676 --> 00:03:30,694
She found the second thread herself.

46
00:03:31,264 --> 00:03:37,470
It had taken her four months, working backward from a
line in a court filing that nobody

47
00:03:37,490 --> 00:03:43,695
else had read — a bill from a private investigation
firm, submitted by Graham Tolliver's

48
00:03:43,715 --> 00:03:49,778
lawyers in the Chancery case and then quietly
withdrawn. Two retired Wilmington police

49
00:03:49,798 --> 00:03:56,003
officers. One was now in Arizona, and hung up the
moment he heard the word Umbrella. The

50
00:03:56,023 --> 00:04:02,299
other was a deacon at a small church in Lancaster
County, and he did not hang up, because

51
00:04:02,319 --> 00:04:08,099
he was a Christian, and he did not believe in
being rude to women on his doorstep.

52
00:04:08,789 --> 00:04:12,392
I won't talk about it, miss. I'm sorry
you came all this way.

53
00:04:12,913 --> 00:04:17,204
I'm not asking you to talk about it, Mister Dorsey.
I'm only asking you one thing. What

54
00:04:17,224 --> 00:04:18,592
did you see in the basement?

55
00:04:20,312 --> 00:04:26,295
He did not answer for a long time. A car went by on
the road, and a dog barked somewhere.

56
00:04:26,985 --> 00:04:31,661
There were two men. They didn't blink. The whole
time. I was a cop for twenty-six years,

57
00:04:31,681 --> 00:04:36,518
miss, and I've seen men on every drug there is, and
I've never seen a man who didn't blink.

58
00:04:37,038 --> 00:04:37,860
And?

59
00:04:38,379 --> 00:04:43,020
And there was a machine in the corner. A big grey
thing, like an air conditioner. And

60
00:04:43,040 --> 00:04:43,679
it breathed.

61
00:04:45,079 --> 00:04:49,717
It breathed, miss. Like something asleep. And there
was a window in it. And something

62
00:04:49,737 --> 00:04:51,033
in there opened its eye.

63
00:04:53,052 --> 00:04:58,457
He went inside and closed the door. She stood on the
step for a while, in the sunshine,

64
00:04:58,477 --> 00:05:01,513
with her notebook open and
nothing written in it.

65
00:05:02,083 --> 00:05:07,892
On the drive home she told herself that he was a
traumatised man, and that trauma distorts

66
00:05:07,912 --> 00:05:13,657
memory, and that a compressor cycle can sound like
breathing if you are frightened enough

67
00:05:13,677 --> 00:05:17,349
in the dark. All of which was true.
None of which helped.

68
00:05:19,418 --> 00:05:23,338
The Director's invitation came
through Tomás, in October.

69
00:05:23,908 --> 00:05:30,200
Full access. That was the offer. Six weeks. Any floor
of the tower, any meeting she wanted

70
00:05:30,220 --> 00:05:36,373
to sit in on, any employee she wanted to interview,
and six hours of the Director's time

71
00:05:36,393 --> 00:05:40,161
in person, spread across the six
weeks, on the record.

72
00:05:40,730 --> 00:05:46,573
She knew exactly what it was. It was the clean face
— every floor he wanted her to see,

73
00:05:46,593 --> 00:05:52,369
every person he wanted her to meet. She knew that
the access was the cage. She took it

74
00:05:52,389 --> 00:05:58,568
anyway, because access is also a door, and she intended
to walk through it and keep walking.

75
00:05:59,139 --> 00:06:05,124
She saw everything they showed her. She saw the assay
programme on the ninth floor, where

76
00:06:05,144 --> 00:06:11,062
the second-generation device — smaller, faster,
forty-six pathogens now instead of forty

77
00:06:11,082 --> 00:06:17,134
— was being prepared for launch. She saw the Aeterna
trial ward, and met Arthur Kowalczyk,

78
00:06:17,154 --> 00:06:23,275
eighty years old now, who shook her hand with a grip
that hurt and told her he was learning

79
00:06:23,295 --> 00:06:24,692
to ride a motorcycle.

80
00:06:25,262 --> 00:06:30,766
She saw Evelyn Marsh, who gave her forty minutes and
said nothing at all, beautifully.

81
00:06:31,336 --> 00:06:37,407
She saw Catherine Adeyemi, who gave her an hour and
said a great deal, all of it on the

82
00:06:37,427 --> 00:06:40,628
record, all of it true, all of
it about money.

83
00:06:42,197 --> 00:06:43,930
And she saw the men in black.

84
00:06:44,500 --> 00:06:50,388
She tried to photograph one of them, on the third day,
in a corridor on the eleventh floor

85
00:06:50,408 --> 00:06:56,427
— a tall man with short hair standing outside the
Director's office. She took four pictures.

86
00:06:56,447 --> 00:07:02,007
In all four, his face was smeared, as if he had
moved, although she had been watching

87
00:07:02,027 --> 00:07:04,108
him and he had not moved at all.

88
00:07:04,677 --> 00:07:10,341
She showed Tomás. Tomás laughed and said the
building had a lot of glass and the light

89
00:07:10,361 --> 00:07:15,761
was weird. He said it too quickly. She wrote down
that he had said it too quickly.

90
00:07:17,831 --> 00:07:20,204
The last of the six hours was a dinner.

91
00:07:20,774 --> 00:07:26,570
He chose the restaurant — a quiet place in
Philadelphia with dark wood and a private room

92
00:07:26,590 --> 00:07:32,190
— and he ordered for both of them again, and again
it was what she would have ordered,

93
00:07:32,210 --> 00:07:33,889
and this time she said so.

94
00:07:34,577 --> 00:07:36,233
You do that on purpose.

95
00:07:36,754 --> 00:07:39,806
I do everything on purpose. It's a
character flaw.

96
00:07:40,326 --> 00:07:41,982
How do you know what I'd order?

97
00:07:42,502 --> 00:07:47,730
You told me. The way you read the menu. You looked at
the fish twice and the steak once,

98
00:07:47,750 --> 00:07:52,859
and your eyes went back to the fish, and you have an
espresso habit, so you won't want

99
00:07:52,879 --> 00:07:56,318
a heavy sauce. It isn't magic, Miss
Price. It's attention.

100
00:07:57,838 --> 00:08:02,306
The recorder was on the table between them. He
had asked her to put it there.

101
00:08:02,875 --> 00:08:08,502
They talked for two hours. She asked him about
Verdant, and about Aeterna, and about the

102
00:08:08,522 --> 00:08:14,279
eleven seconds, and he answered every question —
calmly, fluently, charmingly, and without

103
00:08:14,299 --> 00:08:19,734
telling her a single thing she did not already
know. She had interviewed senators who

104
00:08:19,754 --> 00:08:24,163
were worse at it. She had interviewed a
cardinal who was worse at it.

105
00:08:24,733 --> 00:08:30,296
At the end of the second hour, over coffee, she did
what the voice on the phone had told

106
00:08:30,316 --> 00:08:30,931
her to do.

107
00:08:32,620 --> 00:08:34,256
What's Control Seven?

108
00:08:36,277 --> 00:08:42,371
He did not answer immediately. She was watching his
face as closely as she had ever watched

109
00:08:42,391 --> 00:08:45,194
anything, exactly as she had been told to.

110
00:08:45,764 --> 00:08:51,592
She saw nothing. That was what she would write in
her notes afterwards. Nothing at all.

111
00:08:51,612 --> 00:08:57,576
Not a flicker, not a pause in his breathing, not a
movement of his hands. The face across

112
00:08:57,596 --> 00:09:01,072
the table did not change by so much
as a millimetre.

113
00:09:01,642 --> 00:09:07,348
It was, she realised later, the most frightening
thing she had ever seen. Because every

114
00:09:07,368 --> 00:09:12,942
human face changes when it is surprised. And his
had not — which could only mean that

115
00:09:12,962 --> 00:09:18,932
he had not been surprised. Which could only mean that
he had known the question was coming.

116
00:09:21,121 --> 00:09:26,661
It's a calibration sample. Howard Lessing ran it in
the autumn of twenty twenty-six, and

117
00:09:26,681 --> 00:09:31,968
it broke an analyzer, and he's never forgiven me
for being right about the reagents.

118
00:09:33,288 --> 00:09:36,317
He had said Howard Lessing's
name. She had not.

119
00:09:36,888 --> 00:09:42,176
She understood, sitting in the candlelight with the
rain on the tall windows, that he

120
00:09:42,196 --> 00:09:47,734
had just told her he knew exactly who had called her.
And she understood, a second later,

121
00:09:47,754 --> 00:09:50,233
that he had wanted her to understand it.

122
00:09:52,423 --> 00:09:53,614
Who are you?

123
00:09:54,134 --> 00:09:59,339
It was not a journalist's question. It came out of
her before she could stop it, in a

124
00:09:59,359 --> 00:10:02,229
different voice than the one she
used for work.

125
00:10:03,919 --> 00:10:09,105
I'll tell you one true thing, Hannah. Just one. And
then you can write whatever you like.

126
00:10:10,304 --> 00:10:15,809
I was born in September twenty twenty-six.
Everything before that is paperwork.

127
00:10:17,830 --> 00:10:23,721
She wrote it down. Of course she wrote it down. It was
the best line in the whole profile,

128
00:10:23,741 --> 00:10:29,435
and she used it as the last line, and when the
magazine came out, everybody who read it

129
00:10:29,455 --> 00:10:35,018
took it the way she had taken it at the table: as a
metaphor. A self-made man's boast

130
00:10:35,038 --> 00:10:40,733
about reinvention. The kind of thing a charismatic
chief executive says to a journalist

131
00:10:40,753 --> 00:10:44,476
over coffee, because he knows it will
look good in print.

132
00:10:45,047 --> 00:10:48,979
It was the only completely true thing he
told her in six weeks.

133
00:10:51,048 --> 00:10:53,114
She got home at twenty past one.

134
00:10:53,682 --> 00:10:59,237
The apartment was exactly as she had left it. The
door had been locked. The window had

135
00:10:59,257 --> 00:11:02,996
been latched. The cat was on the bed.
Nothing was missing.

136
00:11:03,567 --> 00:11:07,836
Her notebook was on her desk, where she had
left it, beside the laptop.

137
00:11:08,405 --> 00:11:11,317
It was one inch to the left of
where she had left it.

138
00:11:11,888 --> 00:11:17,265
She knew it was one inch because she had a habit,
from her years on the courts beat, of

139
00:11:17,285 --> 00:11:22,786
squaring her notebook against the edge of the desk
when she left it, and it was no longer

140
00:11:22,806 --> 00:11:28,370
square. It had been moved. Carefully, and not
carefully enough. Or — she thought, standing

141
00:11:28,390 --> 00:11:33,953
in the doorway with her keys in her hand and her heart
going very fast — exactly carefully

142
00:11:33,973 --> 00:11:34,388
enough.

143
00:11:36,458 --> 00:11:41,934
She did not call the police. There was nothing to
report. A notebook, moved one inch.

144
00:11:42,504 --> 00:11:47,348
She sat up for the rest of the night at her kitchen
table with the lights on and the cat

145
00:11:47,368 --> 00:11:52,046
in her lap, and she understood something that would
stay with her for the rest of her

146
00:11:52,066 --> 00:11:56,798
career: that for two years she had been following a
story, and for two years the story

147
00:11:56,818 --> 00:12:01,496
had been following her back. That it had smelled
her the way she had smelled it. That

148
00:12:01,516 --> 00:12:06,415
it knew her rent, and her ex-boyfriend's name, and
the fact that she squared her notebook

149
00:12:06,435 --> 00:12:08,018
against the edge of the desk.

150
00:12:08,587 --> 00:12:10,558
And that it had wanted her to know.

151
00:12:12,628 --> 00:12:18,361
The profile ran in January. It was twelve thousand
words long. It was, everyone agreed,

152
00:12:18,381 --> 00:12:24,180
the best thing ever written about Albert Wesker. It
was fair, and sharp, and beautifully

153
00:12:24,200 --> 00:12:29,801
written, and it said that he was the most
charismatic chief executive in America, and

154
00:12:29,821 --> 00:12:33,835
that he was hiding something, and that
she did not know what.

155
00:12:34,404 --> 00:12:39,599
It did not mention Control Seven. It did not
mention a machine that breathed.

156
00:12:40,168 --> 00:12:44,639
She told her editor she did not have enough to
stand it up. That was true.

157
00:12:45,210 --> 00:12:46,824
It was not the reason.
